ghazi muhammad's family is in mourning  the 24 year old from mad medini in  sudan's al jazeera state was killed  during an anti-military protest a week  ago  i was in khartoum someone called me and  said your brother was hit by a tear gas  canister  i called those who are with him and they  told me he had had a small injury when  in reality he was hit by a bullet to the  head  then i got a call from someone who gave  me his condolences  that's when i knew my brother had died  gassing was the one who supported this  family he dropped out of school to drive  an auto rickshaw whenever i called him  he was either at a funeral or at  weddings helping people  al-jazeera state has witnessed large  protests since the military took over  power in october dissolving a two-year  transitional government which was meant  to lead sudan to democracy security  forces have repeatedly used tear gas and  live ammunition against demonstrators  at least 78 protesters have been killed  and more than two and a half thousand  injured  with medany may not be the only city  besides the capital khartoum where  thousands have taken to the streets to  protest against military rule but its  location in central sudan is significant  it shows that the demand for civilian  rule and democracy transcends the social  and ethnic barriers that used to  dominate sudan's political landscape  for decades sudan's central region was  more stable than other areas which  suffered from civil wars but since  december 2018 when sudan's revolution  began many more people in cities closer  to the capital joined the  anti-government movement that movement  was reignited when the military took  control  the protest movement in wad madani is  not less than what is happening in  khartoum when two protesters from here  were killed including gasim people came  from all over the state and from  neighboring states we want to see the  future of those protesters who died  envisioned we want a democratic united  sudan  ghaziam's family say it may be painful  but they take comfort knowing he died  demanding change and to see a better  country hiba morgan al-jazeera with  madani sudan
 

